# Philosophy

@lede
Brief is a refusal. Not of Markdown — of what Markdown became after
twenty years of accreted dialects.
@end

## The problem Markdown solved, briefly

Markdown's original 2004 design was elegant: plain-text input, easy to
read in a terminal, renders to HTML. It became the lingua franca of
README files, Stack Overflow answers, and every chat tool with a code
block button.

That elegance came from *forgivingness*. The parser would accept almost
any input and produce a best-effort rendering. For its original use case
— individual humans writing short documents — this was correct.

## The problem Markdown introduced

Forgivingness compounds badly across:

@dl
Multiple renderers
: GitHub-Flavored Markdown, CommonMark, MultiMarkdown, Discord's flavor,
  Slack's flavor, Notion's variant. The same source produces different
  output depending on the consumer. There is no single Markdown.
Long documents
: A 100-page book in Markdown collects a long tail of "this looks fine
  but renders weird in some renderers" bugs. The forgiveness that helped
  short documents now hurts.
LLM consumers
: Every token costs money. Forgiving parsers preserve typographic noise
  the model has to weigh equally with semantic content. There is no
  affordance for "this is a prompt; squeeze it."
Tooling
: Without strict syntax, every tool reimplements the parser, and every
  tool has its own bugs. There is no canonical AST to share.
@end

The story of Markdown's success is a story of network effects winning
over correctness. Brief is an attempt to take the second turn.

## The design rules

@features
@feature(title: "One way to do it")
Every construct has a single canonical spelling. If two surface forms
produce the same AST, only one is legal. The compiler refuses ambiguous
input rather than guessing.
@end

@feature(title: "Errors over guesses")
When input is ambiguous, the compiler emits an error with a span and a
code. It never silently picks a winner. There is no `--allow-warnings`
flag.
@end

@feature(title: "Tokens are a first-class cost")
Source-level brevity wins over visual symmetry where the two conflict.
The LLM target is part of the compiler, not bolted on.
@end

@feature(title: "No inline HTML, ever")
Extension goes through the shortcode system. The parser stays
context-free; non-HTML targets get a real translation, not a
passthrough.
@end
@end

## What this means in practice

A markup language whose extension story is "drop in arbitrary HTML"
trades determinism for flexibility. Brief makes the opposite trade.
Every shortcode is registered, every argument is typed, every output
target gets its own template.

A markup language whose error model is "warnings" trades correctness for
convenience. Brief makes the opposite trade. Every diagnostic is an
error; the compiler has two outcomes (success or failure); the formatter
fixes most "warnings" automatically.

A markup language whose AST is "whatever pulldown-cmark returned this
release" trades stability for shipping speed. Brief makes the opposite
trade. The grammar is small, the AST is published, and the compiler
emits stable spans into both.

## Compared to other strict languages

Brief is closest in spirit to *gofmt*, *Black*, and *rustc*: there is one
canonical form, and the compiler tells you when you've drifted. It is
*not* close in spirit to LaTeX (heavy syntax, Turing-complete macros) or
restructured text (rich grammar, multiple renderers).

The shortcode system is the explicit acknowledgment that *some*
extension is necessary. The constraint is that extensions are
*registered*, *validated*, and have *templates per target*. Anything
less and we are back to inline HTML.

## What we lose

Brief is more verbose than Markdown for some constructs (callouts,
images). It refuses input that "obviously means" something a forgiving
parser would render. It requires a `brief.toml` for cross-document
references. It has a learning curve, where Markdown has none.

These are trades, not bugs. If you measure "minutes to get a single
short document rendered," Brief loses to Markdown. If you measure
"minutes spent across the lifetime of a corpus understanding why two
renderers disagree on the same source," Brief is the cheaper option.

## What we gain

@dl
Determinism
: The same source always produces the same output. Across renderers,
  across compiler versions, across machines.
A real diagnostic story
: Errors with spans and codes that you can grep, file as bugs, and fix
  systematically. `brief explain B0204` instead of "the rendering
  looked wrong."
Token economy
: An LLM-mode rendering that is part of the compiler, not bolted on.
  Real measurement, real budgeting.
A small AST
: Tooling that wants to operate on Brief documents has a stable,
  published AST. No "but which Markdown dialect" question.
@end

## Status

The grammar is stable. The compiler ships what is documented on this
site; anything beyond that is unstable.
